      "bill": "both",
      "taught": true,
      "bonus": true,
      "whatExisted": "The delta rule, three years old, correct, and slow to train because each update depends on the previous one.",
      "problem": "A sequential recurrence cannot use the parallelism that made transformers trainable, so a good idea stayed impractical at scale.",
      "mechanism": "Reformulate the delta-rule recurrence as a matrix product that can be chunked, so a whole block of tokens is updated in parallel rather than one at a time.",
      "whatItFixed": "Delta-rule models became trainable at competitive scale, which is what moved the idea from a paper into architectures.",
      "newTradeoff": "The chunked form is more complex to implement and reason about, and it does not change what the state can hold - the recall limits of a fixed-size state are untouched.",
      "buys": "Practical training speed for an update rule that was already known to be better.",
      "givesUp": "Implementation simplicity; the arithmetic is no longer something you can read off in a line.",
      "whenToChoose": "Any serious use of the delta rule at scale - this is the version that made it deployable.",
